# A test run has opened `get` on Object.prototype. A member that a written descriptor leaves out is looked for above it, so `{ value, enumerable, configurable, writable }` finds what lies there:
# what the body lays is not all that the call reads off the descriptor. It leaves like any other, and the definition stays one by a descriptor that nobody has read
# function K(v) { this.v = v; }      Object.defineProperty(K.prototype, "m", { value: function m() { return this.v; }, enumerable: false, configurable: true, writable: true });
# The records are those of the twin, ../data-descriptor-in-sight-is-a-store.ir, where the function lies on the prototype object as behind a store, and one more: the last line.
# The callee is written as the library writes it: in a test run nobody takes `Object.defineProperty` of the program's own files for the built-in.
# The walk asks name by name: walk/builtin-calls/written-descriptor-below-a-name-that-a-test-run-has-opened-is-read-by-the-built-in.ir
# OPTIONS: admit=ReplacedBuiltins
# RUN: link members layouts mechanisms=all
# CHECK: on-prototype L0 "m"
# RUN(STORE): link members layouts mechanisms=all
# STORE-NOT: on-prototype L0 "m" function=

